#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "5d2c0fff-c9af-5e8a-846a-640878f08283" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-730" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{u} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 4} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x}{x^{2} - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7df7fed3-c172-5cc2-aca8-abd31a0902a0" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-731" :question/problem "If $y = \\log_2(\\ln{x})$, what is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\log_2 u, \\qquad u = \\ln x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u\\ln 2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{u\\ln 2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{xu\\ln 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x\\ln x\\ln 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
